  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nStorm Chances: A narrow band of showers and thunderstorms is \ndeveloping across central WI early this morning along a slow moving \ncold front. As this front continues east across the area expect \ncontinued scattered storm development from central to far \nnortheast WI through mid morning before the region drys out. \nBehind the front a much drier air mass will move into the region \nleaving mostly dry conditions for the remainder of the work week. \nNext chance (60-70%) for showers/storms comes late Friday into \nSaturday as an upper trough drags another cold front across the \nregion. While a few stronger elevated storms capable of producing \nhail and 40-50 mph wind gusts are possible with 500-800 J/kg of \nMUCAPE and 30-35kts of deep-layer shear, a strong cap around 800mb\nwill limit the potential for surface based storms and the risk \nfor severe storms. \n\nGlobal ensembles are more impressed with the severe/heavy rain \npotential on Monday as a seasonally strong upper jet streak (55- \n60kts) overspreads a moist and unstable airmass. There is still a \nlot of uncertainty with the details for Monday, but it will be a \nperiod of monitor, especially for those with outdoor plans.\n\nWindy Today: A tightening pressure gradient and deep mixing today \nwill lead to breezy west to northwest winds. Strongest gusts of 25-\n35 mph are expected this afternoon. As the pressure gradient \nrelaxes winds will trend lighter Thursday.\n\nTemperatures Trends: Temperatures will remain a few degrees warmer \nthan normal today before becoming more seasonal Thursday. \nTemperatures will then trend near to slightly above normal through \nthe weekend and into early next week. There is some signal for a \ncooler period arriving towards the end of next week.",
